#pragma once

#include "/Plugin/CloudAtmosphere/Private/AtmosphereOccluders.ush"

// The shadow map: its encoding, and the two sides that share it.
//
// MODEL-INDEPENDENT. Everything the map's geometry needs travels in
// AtmoShadowFrame -- a shell radius, a depth unit and the cascade half-widths --
// so a model contributes three numbers and inherits the format, the
// reconstruction, the cascade walk and the occluder composite unchanged. The
// alternative, a reader per model, is four copies of a nonlinear reconstruction
// drifting apart from the day they are written.
//
// INCLUDED BY BOTH THE BAKE AND THE MARCH. Basis, extent and the depth a sample
// sits at are derived here from the frame, which both sides already have; a
// second derivation can disagree, and a map sampled in a basis it was not
// written in fails silently, the shadows staying smooth and plausible in the
// wrong place.
//
// THE ENCODING IS INVERTED -- depths at which fixed optical depths are crossed,
// not optical depth at fixed depths. That places the nodes by transmittance, so
// they concentrate where the medium is dense: the top of the density ramp, where
// view transmittance is spent and where a slab fit anchored at the shell entry
// is worst. A deck top with near-zero density breaks the classic form.
//
// TWO BANDS OF SLICES. The first ATMO_SHADOW_CASCADES hold the medium's crossing
// depths; the second hold geometry occlusion, one texel-aligned slice per
// cascade, added to the optical depth at read time. Kept apart because the
// crossing encoding cannot express partial occlusion -- see
// AtmosphereOccluders.ush -- and because a blocker is not a medium.
//
// DEPTHS ARE OFFSETS FROM THE ANALYTIC SHELL ENTRY, in thicknesses, so fp16
// resolves a fraction of a gradient rather than of a planet radius, and the
// entry is recomputed in full precision on each side. Optical depth is on the
// FASTEST ABSORPTION CHANNEL, the largest extinction tint folded in by the bake,
// so the thresholds mean transmittance directly.

/** The four stored crossings, spaced by what transmittance does rather than
 *  evenly: 0.25 is a 22% loss, 1.0 is 63%, 3.0 is 95%. The second is low on
 *  purpose, resolving the soft top of the ramp -- what the eye reads and what a
 *  linear fit gets worst. THE FIRST IS THE SILHOUETTE, a threshold rather than a
 *  geometric entry: a cone-traced deck top is a point sample of one column, so
 *  it cannot be averaged over the texel and snaps by a whole one as the medium
 *  advects past the lattice. As a crossing of the same subsampled accumulation
 *  as the others, it moves continuously. */
#ifndef ATMO_SHADOW_TAU_ENTRY
#define ATMO_SHADOW_TAU_ENTRY 0.02f
#endif

#ifndef ATMO_SHADOW_TAU0
#define ATMO_SHADOW_TAU0 0.25f
#endif

#ifndef ATMO_SHADOW_TAU1
#define ATMO_SHADOW_TAU1 1.0f
#endif

#ifndef ATMO_SHADOW_TAU2
#define ATMO_SHADOW_TAU2 3.0f
#endif

/** Optical depth at which the tail extrapolation stops growing, on the most
 *  transmissive term the caller will exponentiate. Under a percent at 5. A
 *  CEILING, NOT A CUTOFF: past the last node the reconstruction continues at the
 *  final segment's rate, unbounded on a dense column. Never use it to snap
 *  transmittance to zero -- a hard threshold on a texel-interpolated field draws
 *  an axis-aligned staircase.
 *
 *  PITFALL: the reader sees the map's depth scaled down, by each slower
 *  channel's share of the fastest and by any multiple-scattering attenuation, so
 *  a ceiling on the fastest channel leaves those glowing through saturated
 *  medium. The caller divides this by the smallest such scale. */
#ifndef ATMO_SHADOW_SATURATION
#define ATMO_SHADOW_SATURATION 5.0f
#endif

/** Floor on that scale. A channel or octave the medium barely attenuates cannot
 *  be made opaque by any finite ceiling; this caps the ceiling instead. */
#ifndef ATMO_SHADOW_MIN_TAU_SCALE
#define ATMO_SHADOW_MIN_TAU_SCALE 1e-3f
#endif

/** Written where a threshold is never reached, and where the ray misses the
 *  shell. Far past any real chord, so reconstruction reads zero rather than
 *  needing a validity test. */
#ifndef ATMO_SHADOW_NO_DECK
#define ATMO_SHADOW_NO_DECK 1000.0f
#endif

/** How far the map reaches past the cull radius. The limb is where the encoded
 *  depths vary fastest, and a map cut exactly at the shell puts that variation
 *  against the clamp edge with nothing outside to blend toward. Applied by
 *  whichever model builds the frame, not here. */
#ifndef ATMO_SHADOW_EXTENT_MARGIN
#define ATMO_SHADOW_EXTENT_MARGIN 1.02f
#endif

/** Lookup offset, in texels, applied per view sample. 1 is a full texel, larger
 *  softens the shadow, 0 disables. The opposite case to the bake, where a
 *  stochastic offset would print as fixed noise: here it is per pixel and per
 *  sample, so one ray's samples average the texel neighbourhood. It earns its
 *  place in the tangent case, where a view ray parallel to the light projects
 *  every sample into nearly one texel and that texel's error reaches the screen
 *  as a block. */
#ifndef ATMO_SHADOW_LOOKUP_JITTER
#define ATMO_SHADOW_LOOKUP_JITTER 1.0f
#endif

/** Angular warp on cascade 0, as a fraction of a quarter turn: 1 stores the
 *  disc against the angle from the sub-light point, 0 against the planar
 *  offset. BAKE AND READ BOTH TAKE IT FROM HERE, and a map read with a warp it
 *  was not baked with lands every shadow at the wrong radius.
 *
 *  WHY. A planar texel covers 1 / cos(angle) of ground along the light, so
 *  toward the terminator each texel's error is drawn out into a streak along
 *  the light. At 1 the ground resolution is uniform across the lit hemisphere:
 *  7 times finer at 85 degrees and 18 at 88, pi/2 coarser under the light.
 *  The price is bake time: more texels land on the long grazing chords. */
#ifndef ATMO_SHADOW_DISC_WARP
#define ATMO_SHADOW_DISC_WARP 1.0f
#endif

/** Cascade count. Level 0 covers the whole disc; each inner level is sized by
 *  the model to match the radius a layer is drawn to, so shadow resolution steps
 *  up where the field gains a layer. Adding one costs a slice and a case in
 *  whatever builds the frame. */
#ifndef ATMO_SHADOW_CASCADES
#define ATMO_SHADOW_CASCADES 3
#endif

/** Where a cascade starts handing over to the one outside it, as a fraction of
 *  its half-width. The outer band reconstructs both and blends.
 *
 *  THE SEAM IS NOT THE RESOLUTION CHANGE. Subsample offsets scale with texel
 *  size, so the near map band-limits at a finer scale and reads slightly denser
 *  over the same ground. A narrow band turns that into a ring. */
#ifndef ATMO_SHADOW_NEAR_BLEND
#define ATMO_SHADOW_NEAR_BLEND 0.75f
#endif

/** Slice a cascade's occluder term lives in, and the slice count that means a
 *  target has them at all.
 *
 *  THE TARGET'S OWN DEPTH IS THE SWITCH. A target sized to one band has no
 *  occluders, and reading one anyway clamps to the last medium slice and turns
 *  crossing depths into a planet-wide shadow of whatever the medium held. Gating
 *  on the slice count rather than on a define or a parameter means the condition
 *  that makes the read safe is the same condition that enables it, and there is
 *  no second place to keep in step. */
#define AtmoShadow_OccluderSlice(Level) (float(ATMO_SHADOW_CASCADES) + float(Level))

#define ATMO_SHADOW_SLICES_WITH_OCCLUDERS (2 * ATMO_SHADOW_CASCADES)

/** Occluder blur radius IN THICKNESSES, and how many taps it is spent over.
 *
 *  NOT IN TEXELS. A texel spans an order of magnitude more ground on the disc
 *  slice than on the detail slice, so a width in texels makes every cascade
 *  produce a differently sized shadow, and the walk crossfading between them
 *  steps outward at each boundary. In world units they agree, down to the point
 *  where a level's own lattice is coarser than the width asked for.
 *
 *  PAIR IT WITH THE BAKE'S FOOTPRINT, the same quantity in the same units on the
 *  capture side: the bake erodes by it and this pushes back out by it, so the
 *  two soften the edge without moving it. Blurring an uneroded edge grows a halo.
 *
 *  FILTERED HERE, NOT IN THE BAKE. Coverage in the bake is a fraction of its tap
 *  count, so widening it there quantises the edge into as many steps as it has
 *  taps. These taps are bilinear and land anywhere, so the result is continuous.
 *
 *  Taps cost a fetch each, per cascade, per light sample. At most 8, since that
 *  is what the offset table holds. */
#ifndef ATMO_SHADOW_OCCLUDER_BLUR
#define ATMO_SHADOW_OCCLUDER_BLUR 0.002f
#endif

#ifndef ATMO_SHADOW_OCCLUDER_TAPS
#define ATMO_SHADOW_OCCLUDER_TAPS 8
#endif

/** Everything about a map's geometry that is not in the map.
 *
 *  THE MODEL'S ONLY CONTRIBUTION. A shell radius, the depth unit every stored
 *  depth is expressed in, and the cascade half-widths. Both sides build one from
 *  their own field and nothing below this line knows which model it came from. */
struct AtmoShadowFrame
{
	/** Radius of the shell the map covers, BEFORE the extent margin. The chord
	 *  the depth axis is anchored to, so both sides must use the same one. */
    float CullRadius;

	/** Depth unit. Stored and reconstructed depths are multiples of it. */
    float Thickness;

	/** Half-width per cascade, outermost first. Must be non-increasing: a fine
	 *  level wider than a coarse one puts the coarse slice inside it, which
	 *  reads as the fine slice not working. Zero collapses a level and the walk
	 *  skips it. */
    float Extents[ATMO_SHADOW_CASCADES];
};

/** The smallest and largest channel across the bands' extinction tints.
 *
 *  THE FOLD CONSTANT: the map stores optical depth scaled by the largest channel
 *  any band can have, so its thresholds mean transmittance on the fastest
 *  channel anywhere, and the reader divides it back out and applies the
 *  receiving band's tint. The smallest channel sets the saturation ceiling. */
float2 AtmoShadow_ExtinctionTintRange(float3 TintNegative, float3 TintPositive, float3 TintBase)
{
    const float3 Lo = min(TintNegative, min(TintPositive, TintBase));
    const float3 Hi = max(TintNegative, max(TintPositive, TintBase));

    return float2(min(Lo.x, min(Lo.y, Lo.z)), max(Hi.x, max(Hi.y, Hi.z)));
}

/** The map plane's axes for a planet-local light direction, orthonormal and
 *  perpendicular to it. L must already be normalized.
 *
 *  ANCHORED TO THE SPIN AXIS rather than an arbitrary perpendicular, so the grid
 *  does not rotate as the light moves: a rotating lattice re-phases the
 *  reconstruction filter every frame and shimmers at the resolution limit even
 *  when nothing has moved. The axis lands on V, putting the pole up the map.
 *  (U, V, L) IS RIGHT-HANDED -- the other cross order mirrors the map, which is
 *  invisible on a banded planet and surfaces later as shadows on the wrong side
 *  of the lumps casting them. */
void AtmoShadow_Basis(float3 L, out float3 OutU, out float3 OutV)
{
    float3 V = float3(0.0f, 0.0f, 1.0f) - L * L.z;

	// Light along the pole. Any perpendicular is as stable as any other there.
    if (dot(V, V) < 1e-6f)
    {
        V = float3(1.0f, 0.0f, 0.0f) - L * L.x;
    }

    OutV = normalize(V);
    OutU = cross(OutV, L);
}

/** The warp's angle: how far round the sphere the edge of the disc map sits. */
float AtmoShadow_WarpAngle()
{
    return saturate(ATMO_SHADOW_DISC_WARP) * (0.5f * PI);
}

/** Cascade 0's map coordinate, [-1, 1] across the map, to its plane offset over
 *  the extent. Radius sin(A S) / sin(A) at map radius S: the planar projection
 *  of a sphere sampled evenly in angle. Past S = 1 it holds the edge, which is
 *  outside the shell. */
float2 AtmoShadow_DiscToPlane(float2 Q)
{
    const float A = AtmoShadow_WarpAngle();
    const float S = length(Q);

    if (A < 1e-3f)
    {
        return Q;
    }

    if (S < 1e-6f)
    {
        return Q * (A / sin(A));
    }

    return Q * (sin(A * min(S, 1.0f)) / (S * sin(A)));
}

/** The inverse: plane offset over the extent to cascade 0's map coordinate. */
float2 AtmoShadow_PlaneToDisc(float2 P)
{
    const float A = AtmoShadow_WarpAngle();
    const float R = length(P);

    if (A < 1e-3f)
    {
        return P;
    }

    if (R < 1e-6f)
    {
        return P * (sin(A) / A);
    }

    return P * (asin(min(R * sin(A), 1.0f)) / (A * R));
}

/** The plane offset one map-coordinate step moves along each map axis, over
 *  the extent: the columns of AtmoShadow_DiscToPlane's Jacobian. Radial and
 *  tangential scales differ, so a texel's footprint is an ellipse. */
void AtmoShadow_DiscJacobian(float2 Q, out float2 OutX, out float2 OutY)
{
    const float A = AtmoShadow_WarpAngle();
    const float S = length(Q);

    if (A < 1e-3f)
    {
        OutX = float2(1.0f, 0.0f);
        OutY = float2(0.0f, 1.0f);
        return;
    }

    const float InvSinA = 1.0f / sin(A);
    const float Clamped = min(S, 1.0f);

    const float Radial = A * cos(A * Clamped) * InvSinA;
    const float Tangential = (S < 1e-6f) ? A * InvSinA : sin(A * Clamped) / S * InvSinA;

    const float2 N = (S < 1e-6f) ? float2(1.0f, 0.0f) : Q / S;
    const float Cross = Radial - Tangential;

    OutX = float2(Tangential + Cross * N.x * N.x, Cross * N.x * N.y);
    OutY = float2(Cross * N.x * N.y, Tangential + Cross * N.y * N.y);
}

/** Centre of a cascade in the map plane, snapped to its own texel grid. Level 0
 *  is centred on the planet and never moves. SNAPPING IS LOAD-BEARING: the
 *  centre follows the camera, so an unsnapped map slides its lattice under the
 *  medium and every crossing depth moves with it -- the crawl an anchored march
 *  lattice exists to prevent, by another route. Whole-texel jumps land on
 *  boundaries that already exist. */
float2 AtmoShadow_CascadeCentre(
	int Level, float3 CameraLocal, float3 U, float3 V, float Extent, float2 MapSize)
{
    if (Level <= 0)
    {
        return float2(0.0f, 0.0f);
    }

    const float2 Plane = float2(dot(CameraLocal, U), dot(CameraLocal, V));

    const float2 TexelSize = 2.0f * Extent / max(MapSize, 1.0f);

    return floor(Plane / TexelSize) * TexelSize;
}

/** Optical depth from one texel's nodes at a given depth. Piecewise linear
 *  through the four nodes, monotone by construction since they are sorted by the
 *  depth they were crossed at. CONTINUOUS AT BOTH ENDS: below the entry node the
 *  first segment's rate runs backwards to zero rather than clamping, since the
 *  node marks where optical depth reached ATMO_SHADOW_TAU_ENTRY and clamping
 *  steps that whole threshold on at the silhouette -- 2% of brightness, along
 *  the edge a god ray draws. Past the last node the final rate continues, capped
 *  by Ceiling. */
float AtmoShadow_TauFromNodes(float4 Node, float Depth, float Ceiling)
{
    const float EntrySpan = max(Node.y - Node.x, 1e-4f);

    if (Depth <= Node.x)
    {
        const float EntryRate = (ATMO_SHADOW_TAU0 - ATMO_SHADOW_TAU_ENTRY) / EntrySpan;

        return max(ATMO_SHADOW_TAU_ENTRY - (Node.x - Depth) * EntryRate, 0.0f);
    }

    if (Depth <= Node.y)
    {
        return lerp(ATMO_SHADOW_TAU_ENTRY, ATMO_SHADOW_TAU0,
			saturate((Depth - Node.x) / EntrySpan));
    }

    if (Depth <= Node.z)
    {
        return lerp(ATMO_SHADOW_TAU0, ATMO_SHADOW_TAU1,
			saturate((Depth - Node.y) / max(Node.z - Node.y, 1e-4f)));
    }

    if (Depth <= Node.w)
    {
        return lerp(ATMO_SHADOW_TAU1, ATMO_SHADOW_TAU2,
			saturate((Depth - Node.z) / max(Node.w - Node.z, 1e-4f)));
    }

    const float TailRate =
		(ATMO_SHADOW_TAU2 - ATMO_SHADOW_TAU1) / max(Node.w - Node.z, 1e-4f);

    return min(ATMO_SHADOW_TAU2 + (Depth - Node.w) * TailRate, Ceiling);
}

/** Optical depth geometry adds at a point, filtered across the blur footprint.
 *  Evaluated per tap rather than averaging the stored terms first, so the test
 *  against each tap's own blocker depth survives the filter. */
float AtmoShadow_OccluderTau(
	Texture2DArray ShadowMap,
	SamplerState ShadowSampler,
	int Level,
	float2 UV,
	float Radius,
	float Depth)
{
    const float Slice = AtmoShadow_OccluderSlice(Level);

    float Sum = 0.0f;

    UNROLL

    for (int Tap = 0; Tap < ATMO_SHADOW_OCCLUDER_TAPS; Tap++)
    {
        const float4 Occluder = ShadowMap.SampleLevel(
			ShadowSampler, float3(UV + AtmoOcc_SubOffsets[Tap] * Radius, Slice), 0.0f);

        Sum += AtmoOcc_Tau(Occluder, Depth);
    }

    return Sum / float(ATMO_SHADOW_OCCLUDER_TAPS);
}

/** Optical depth from one cascade slice, at a point already projected into the
 *  map plane. SAMPLER STATE MUST BE CLAMP ON BOTH AXES: the four taps come from
 *  a floored texel index, reaching one texel outside the map at either edge.
 *  They land on exact centres, so a bilinear sampler returns each texel
 *  unfiltered and the weights below are the only reconstruction applied.
 *
 *  FOUR TEXELS RECONSTRUCTED, THEN BLENDED -- not one reconstruction from
 *  blended nodes. The node depths select which segment a sample falls in, so
 *  interpolating first and branching after puts a slope discontinuity wherever a
 *  segment boundary crosses a texel. Those creases sit on the lattice, looking
 *  like resolution, but their width comes from the optical depth step across the
 *  boundary and does not shrink when texels do. QUINTIC WEIGHTS for the blend,
 *  since bilinear's slope jumps read as facets locked to the same lattice. */
float AtmoShadow_TauSlice(
	Texture2DArray ShadowMap,
	SamplerState ShadowSampler,
	int Level,
	float2 Plane,
	float2 Centre,
	float Extent,
	float Depth,
	float2 JitterTexels,
	float2 MapSize,
	float Ceiling,
	float BlurWorld,
	bool bHasOccluders)
{
	// Cascade 0 is stored warped; see ATMO_SHADOW_DISC_WARP.
    const float2 Relative = (Plane - Centre) / Extent;

    const float2 UV = ((Level == 0) ? AtmoShadow_PlaneToDisc(Relative) : Relative) * 0.5f + 0.5f;

    const float2 InvSize = 1.0f / MapSize;

    const float2 Sample = UV * MapSize - 0.5f + JitterTexels * ATMO_SHADOW_LOOKUP_JITTER;
    const float2 Base = floor(Sample);

    float2 Frac = Sample - Base;
    Frac = Frac * Frac * Frac * (Frac * (Frac * 6.0f - 15.0f) + 10.0f);

    const float3 UV00 = float3((Base + 0.5f) * InvSize, float(Level));

    const float4 N00 = ShadowMap.SampleLevel(ShadowSampler, UV00, 0.0f);
    const float4 N10 = ShadowMap.SampleLevel(ShadowSampler, UV00 + float3(InvSize.x, 0.0f, 0.0f), 0.0f);
    const float4 N01 = ShadowMap.SampleLevel(ShadowSampler, UV00 + float3(0.0f, InvSize.y, 0.0f), 0.0f);
    const float4 N11 = ShadowMap.SampleLevel(ShadowSampler, UV00 + float3(InvSize, 0.0f), 0.0f);

    const float T00 = AtmoShadow_TauFromNodes(N00, Depth, Ceiling);
    const float T10 = AtmoShadow_TauFromNodes(N10, Depth, Ceiling);
    const float T01 = AtmoShadow_TauFromNodes(N01, Depth, Ceiling);
    const float T11 = AtmoShadow_TauFromNodes(N11, Depth, Ceiling);

    const float Medium = lerp(lerp(T00, T10, Frac.x), lerp(T01, T11, Frac.x), Frac.y);

	// A UNIFORM BRANCH, so it costs a scalar test and skips the taps entirely on
	// a target with no occluder band. Those taps triple this function's fetch
	// count, and it runs once per light sample, so a map that never carries an
	// occluder must not pay for them.
    BRANCH

    if (!bHasOccluders)
    {
        return Medium;
    }

	// BILINEAR TAPS AT THE TRUE UV, not the four texel centres the nodes need.
	// The occluder term is linear in what it stores, so the filter may run before
	// the evaluation; the nodes cannot, since the depth selects which segment
	// applies to them.
	//
	// The blur width is a distance, so it becomes a UV radius against this
	// level's own half-width. Floored at half a texel: below that the taps land
	// inside one texel and the bilinear ramp is the only edge there is anyway.
	// On cascade 0 the radius ignores the warp, so the blur narrows toward the
	// terminator by the warp's radial scale.
    const float OccluderRadius = max(
		BlurWorld / max(2.0f * Extent, 1e-4f), 0.5f * InvSize.x);

	// ADDED, NOT CAPPED BY Ceiling. The ceiling bounds an extrapolated tail so a
	// slow channel cannot glow through saturated medium; a blocker is opaque on
	// every channel, and letting it past is what makes solid objects read solid.
    return Medium + AtmoShadow_OccluderTau(
		ShadowMap, ShadowSampler, Level, UV, OccluderRadius, Depth);
}

/** Optical depth between a planet-local position and the light, on the fastest
 *  absorption channel. LightLocal points toward the light. Ceiling bounds the
 *  tail past the last node; see ATMO_SHADOW_SATURATION.
 *
 *  ONE PARAMETERISATION, SEVERAL SCALES: every cascade is the whole field at a
 *  different extent, anchored to the same shell entry, so their depths are
 *  directly comparable. Walked outermost inward, each finer level that contains
 *  the sample replacing the result and blended across its own edge.
 *
 *  THE LEVELS DO NOT DECOMPOSE THE FIELD. Layered density composes as a product,
 *  so optical depths do not add across layers, and the encoding is
 *  depth-of-crossing, nonlinear in density -- even a sound linear split would not
 *  survive it. Each slice carries the complete field.
 *
 *  VALID ANYWHERE INSIDE THE SHELL, not only where the medium is. A point on a
 *  surface below the deck reads the whole column above it, which is what makes
 *  this the same call for shadows cast onto geometry. */
float AtmoShadow_Tau(
	Texture2DArray ShadowMap,
	SamplerState ShadowSampler,
	float3 LocalPos,
	float3 LightLocal,
	float3 CameraLocal,
	float2 JitterTexels,
	float Ceiling,
	AtmoShadowFrame Frame)
{
    const float3 L = normalize(LightLocal);

    float3 U, V;
    AtmoShadow_Basis(L, U, V);

    const float2 Plane = float2(dot(LocalPos, U), dot(LocalPos, V));

	// (U, V, L) is orthonormal, so the in-plane offset IS the ray's closest
	// approach to the centre and its length squared is the perpendicular term.
    const float HalfChordSq = Frame.CullRadius * Frame.CullRadius - dot(Plane, Plane);

    if (HalfChordSq <= 0.0f)
    {
        return 0.0f;
    }

	// Depth from the shell entry, matching what the bake marched from. Shared by
	// every cascade: a property of the geometry, not of any lattice.
    const float Depth = (dot(LocalPos, -L) + sqrt(HalfChordSq)) / Frame.Thickness;

    uint MapWidth, MapHeight, MapSlices;
    ShadowMap.GetDimensions(MapWidth, MapHeight, MapSlices);

    const float2 MapSize = float2(MapWidth, MapHeight);

    const bool bHasOccluders = MapSlices >= uint(ATMO_SHADOW_SLICES_WITH_OCCLUDERS);

	// One width for every cascade, so the shadow is the same size in each and the
	// blend between them has nothing to reveal.
    const float BlurWorld = ATMO_SHADOW_OCCLUDER_BLUR * Frame.Thickness;

    float Tau = AtmoShadow_TauSlice(
		ShadowMap, ShadowSampler, 0, Plane, float2(0.0f, 0.0f),
		Frame.Extents[0], Depth, JitterTexels, MapSize, Ceiling, BlurWorld,
		bHasOccluders);

    UNROLL

    for (int Level = 1; Level < ATMO_SHADOW_CASCADES; Level++)
    {
        const float Extent = Frame.Extents[Level];

        if (Extent <= 0.0f)
        {
            continue;
        }

        const float2 Centre = AtmoShadow_CascadeCentre(
			Level, CameraLocal, U, V, Extent, MapSize);

		// Square support, so the edge measure is the larger axis.
        const float2 Relative = (Plane - Centre) / Extent;
        const float Edge = max(abs(Relative.x), abs(Relative.y));

        if (Edge >= 1.0f)
        {
            continue;
        }

        const float Finer = AtmoShadow_TauSlice(
			ShadowMap, ShadowSampler, Level, Plane, Centre, Extent, Depth,
			JitterTexels, MapSize, Ceiling, BlurWorld, bHasOccluders);

        Tau = lerp(Finer, Tau, smoothstep(ATMO_SHADOW_NEAR_BLEND, 1.0f, Edge));
    }

    return Tau;
}

/** Band-weighted optical depth between a point on OPAQUE GEOMETRY and the light,
 *  with the bake's fold constant divided back out. Zero where the receiver is
 *  outside the shell.
 *
 *  DEPTH, NOT TRANSMITTANCE, mirroring what a light march hands back: the
 *  amounts are in the map and the colour is applied where the depth is read,
 *  since the band coordinate at the receiver only a model can compute.
 *
 *  THE RADIAL GATE IS NOT REDUNDANT. AtmoShadow_Tau tests the ray's
 *  PERPENDICULAR offset, which admits any point laterally inside the disc. One
 *  in front of the planet reads near zero and is harmless; one behind it reads
 *  the ceiling, and would be shadowed for standing behind a planet rather than
 *  under a cloud.
 *
 *  THE BIAS RUNS ALONG THE LIGHT, not along the surface normal, because the
 *  quantity being compared is a depth along the light and no normal is available
 *  here -- so the amount needed grows with surface slope and is authored rather
 *  than derived. It exists for the OCCLUDER BAND: a captured surface holds its
 *  own depth in the occluder slice, so an unbiased receiver evaluates at
 *  Past == 0, the occluder's full peak, and every captured surface shadows
 *  itself everywhere. The deck's own nodes need none, sitting above the
 *  receiver, so with captures off this can be zero.
 *
 *  PITFALL: the bias must clear the occluder lattice's depth quantisation across
 *  one texel, which on a slope is texel width times slope -- kilometres on the
 *  disc cascade. Too much detaches shadows from the ground near the terminator,
 *  where a step along a grazing light travels a long way laterally. Tune there,
 *  not at noon.
 *
 *  JitterTexels IS NORMALLY ZERO HERE. A light ray offsets its lookups because
 *  it has many samples along one ray to average the texel neighbourhood with;
 *  one tap per pixel has nothing to average, so an offset is a dither rather
 *  than a filter, and it lands in transmittance. The quintic reconstruction is
 *  continuous on its own. */
float AtmoShadow_SurfaceOptDepth(
	Texture2DArray ShadowMap,
	SamplerState ShadowSampler,
	float3 LocalPos,
	float3 LightLocal,
	float3 CameraLocal,
	float2 JitterTexels,
	float Ceiling,
	AtmoShadowFrame Frame,
	float FoldConstant,
	float ReceiverBias)
{
    if (dot(LocalPos, LocalPos) > Frame.CullRadius * Frame.CullRadius)
    {
        return 0.0f;
    }

    const float3 L = normalize(LightLocal);

    const float Tau = AtmoShadow_Tau(
		ShadowMap, ShadowSampler, LocalPos + L * (ReceiverBias * Frame.Thickness),
		L, CameraLocal, JitterTexels, Ceiling, Frame);

    return Tau / max(FoldConstant, 1e-8f);
}
